-- ═══ 413 Danish State Scholarships at Aarhus University ═══
UPDATE scholarships SET title = 'Danish State Scholarships at Aarhus University', provider = 'Aarhus University', coverage_type = 'Tuition Waiver', amount_usd_value = 0, deadline = 'No separate scholarship application: apply for an Aarhus University master''s programme by 15 January (summer admission) or 15 September (winter admission) and the university automatically assesses you for a Danish State Scholarship alongside the academic assessment - upload no extra scholarship documents. Scholarship offers arrive with (or shortly after) the admission letter and carry a very short acceptance deadline; at the Faculties of Natural and Technical Sciences the award process runs until the final deadline 1 April (summer) / 1 November (winter), so do not decline an admission offer before those dates if your decision depends on the waiver. Next consideration gate: 15 January 2027 (summer admission).', deadline_date = '2027-01-15', deadline_type = 'fixed', deadline_range_start = NULL, deadline_range_end = NULL, degree_levels = $j$["Master's"]$j$, eligible_majors = $j$["Master's degree programmes at Aarhus University - contents vary by faculty: Faculty of Arts and Aarhus BSS combine the tuition waiver with a monthly living grant; the Faculties of Natural Sciences and Technical Sciences offer tuition waivers only"]$j$, eligible_countries = $j$["Talented applicants from outside Europe who are liable to pay tuition fees (non-EU/EEA/Swiss citizens without an exempting residence permit); EU/EEA/Swiss students cannot receive these scholarships (they have the SU scheme instead)"]$j$, min_gpa = NULL, min_ielts = NULL, financial_need_based = false, merit_based = true, description = $desc$Aarhus University offers a limited number of Danish state scholarships to the most talented applicants from outside Europe. Aarhus University has no scholarship funds of its own for this purpose - the scholarships are financed by the Danish state, and they can only be awarded to applicants who are liable to pay tuition fees. The mechanism is automatic: when you apply for admission to a master's programme by the deadline, the university automatically assesses you for a Danish State Scholarship along with the academic assessment of your qualifications; there is no separate application and you should not upload any additional documentation for the scholarship. What a scholarship contains depends on the faculty: at the Faculty of Arts and at Aarhus BSS it is a tuition fee waiver plus a monthly grant towards living expenses (typically, but not always, for the duration of the master's programme, and the waiver itself can be full or partial), whereas the Faculties of Natural Sciences and Technical Sciences offer tuition fee waivers only (100% waivers there are awarded to the most qualified and talented students; a faculty may instead offer services such as help finding a student job). Recipients of only a waiver must still document sufficient funds for the student residence permit. Selection prioritises excellent academic qualifications and the supply is very small - typically only one or two scholarships are offered on each master's programme, and some faculties place a strategic focus on selected programmes or applicants from specific countries. If you are selected you receive the scholarship offer together with your letter of admission in the application system (with a very short deadline to accept); if not, you receive a tuition fee notification instead. Only master's applicants who applied no later than 15 January (summer admission) or 15 September (winter admission) are considered, and the scholarship is tied to the specific programme - it cannot be transferred to another degree programme at AU.$desc$, requirements = $req$You must be a non-EU/EEA/Swiss applicant liable for tuition fees at Aarhus University (EU/EEA/Swiss students cannot apply for scholarships at AU) and submit your master's application no later than 15 January for summer admission or 15 September for winter admission - consideration is then automatic, with no separate form and no additional documents for the scholarship. Priority goes to applicants with excellent academic qualifications; typically only one or two scholarships exist per master's programme and faculties may add strategic priorities (selected programmes or countries), so no guarantee can be made. The scholarship is bound to the specific master's programme and cannot move with you to another AU programme. Practical conditions that follow from the award form: waiver-only recipients must still prove sufficient self-support funds for the residence permit; monthly grant payments (Arts/Aarhus BSS) start only after arrival in Denmark - paid monthly in advance to a Danish Easy Account (NemKonto) once CPR number and tax card are in place (55% is withheld until the tax card exists; holders from so-called developing countries may be exempt from Danish income tax on the scholarship), and the grant may not cover all living expenses. For Natural and Technical Sciences, the award process continues after the admission letters: offers can arrive throughout March up to the final deadline of 1 April (summer) or 1 November (winter) - if your decision depends on the waiver, do not decline the admission offer before those dates; beyond them, full tuition is due. No numeric GPA cutoff and no scholarship-level IELTS figure are published (English documentation belongs to admission), so min_gpa/min_ielts are not recorded; the living-grant's monthly DKK amount is not published on the scheme pages either, so no amount is stored.$req$, website_url = 'https://masters.au.dk/scholarships-and-grants', source_url = 'https://masters.au.dk/scholarships-and-grants', application_url = 'https://masters.au.dk/how-to-apply', recurrence = 'annual', application_status = 'upcoming', verification_status = 'verified', source_reliability = 10, is_active = true, last_verified_at = now(), last_updated_at = now(), notes = $note$REWRITTEN 2026-10-28 (cleanup pass 113) from Aarhus University's official pages fetched the same day - 'Scholarships and grants' on masters.au.dk (Danish state scholarships for the most talented applicants from outside Europe; financed by the Danish state, AU has no own scholarship funds; only for tuition-fee-liable applicants; automatic assessment with the admission application, no separate application or extra documents; content varies - Faculty of Arts and Aarhus BSS: tuition waiver plus a monthly grant towards living expenses, typically but not always for the programme duration; Faculties of Natural and Technical Sciences: tuition waiver only, 100% waivers to the most qualified, faculty may instead offer services such as student-job help; waiver can be full or partial; waiver-only holders must still document self-support for the residence permit; consideration requires applying by 15 January (summer) or 15 September (winter); priority to excellent academic qualifications, typically one or two scholarships per programme, possible strategic focus on programmes/countries; offer arrives with the admission letter under a very short acceptance deadline; Natural/Technical award process continues until final deadlines 1 April/1 November - do not decline admission before then; scholarship tied to the specific programme; EU/EEA/Swiss excluded) and 'Payment of monthly grants' (monthly scholarship paid monthly in advance to a Danish NemKonto after CPR/tax-card formalities, first payment in the first or second month of the semester, 55% tax withheld until a tax card exists, possible tax exemption for holders from so-called developing countries, grant may not cover all living expenses, AU grants no further assistance). The previously stored URL (international.au.dk/.../danish-state-scholarships) returns 404 - the scheme now lives on masters.au.dk. Title normalised from 'Aarhus Danish State Scholarships' to the official scheme naming. Award stored as NULL (DKK, 'year', 'variable'): fee waivers whose krone value follows each programme's fee, plus an unpublished monthly living grant at two faculties - no amount is printed on the fetched pages and none was invented. deadline_date 2027-01-15 is the next consideration gate (the 15 January summer-admission application deadline; the 15 September winter gate is in the rounds JSON); status 'upcoming' because on the verification date the application portal for the summer intake was not shown as already open. min_gpa/min_ielts NULL. university_scholarships row 183 synced; source 2489 repointed from the bare au.dk domain to the live official page. Previous record held the scheme at reliability 4 behind a dead URL with no terms read.$note$, award_amount = NULL, award_currency = 'DKK', award_period = 'year', award_basis = 'variable', living_allowance = NULL, funding_type = 'State-funded tuition waiver (full or partial), at the Faculty of Arts and Aarhus BSS combined with a monthly grant towards living expenses for (typically) the programme duration; Faculties of Natural and Technical Sciences award tuition waivers only. Typically 1-2 scholarships per master''s programme; no separate application - assessed automatically with admission.', tuition_coverage = 'The tuition fee is waived in whole (the Natural/Technical Sciences waivers are 100%) or in part depending on the award; living costs are only supported at Arts/Aarhus BSS via the monthly grant, and even then the grant may not cover everything (residence-permit funds still required for waiver-only holders).', english_requirements = 'No scholarship-level IELTS figure; English documentation is part of the master''s admission requirements of the chosen programme.', required_documents = $j$["Aarhus University master's application submitted by 15 January (summer admission) or 15 September (winter admission) - consideration for the scholarship is automatic", "No separate scholarship application and no additional documents for the scholarship itself (AU expressly asks applicants not to upload any)", "After an award with a monthly grant: Danish CPR number, Easy Account (NemKonto) and tax card for payment; scholarship letter needed at the tax centre"]$j$, expected_opening_period = NULL, expected_deadline_period = NULL, rounds = $json$[{"intake": "Summer admission", "apply_by": "2027-01-15", "final_waiver_decision": "2027-04-01", "status": "upcoming"}, {"intake": "Winter admission", "apply_by": "2027-09-15", "final_waiver_decision": "2027-11-01", "status": "upcoming"}, {"pattern": "Two gates every year: master's application by 15 January (summer) or 15 September (winter); scholarship assessed automatically with admission; Natural/Technical Sciences awards can continue until 1 April / 1 November"}]$json$ WHERE id = 413;

-- ═══ 377 RETIRE (invented name; vacancy board) ═══
UPDATE scholarships SET is_active = false, application_status = 'closed', award_amount = NULL, award_currency = NULL, award_period = NULL, award_basis = NULL, living_allowance = NULL, funding_type = NULL, tuition_coverage = NULL, english_requirements = NULL, required_documents = NULL, expected_opening_period = NULL, expected_deadline_period = NULL, rounds = NULL, notes = $note$Retired on 2026-10-28 (cleanup pass 113) as an invented-name row pointing at an employment-vacancy board. There is no programme called the 'UCPH International PhD Scholarship' at the University of Copenhagen: like everywhere in Denmark, a UCPH doctorate is normally an employment relationship - PhD fellows are members of scientific staff hired under the Collective Agreement for Academics in the State (per UCPH's own International Staff Mobility salary pages, which list the PhD fellow as an employment category with basic salary, a centrally agreed supplement and pension, and per the Danish unions' terms: typically 37-hour employment with up to 840 hours of assigned duties over three years) - and funded PhD positions are advertised one by one, each with its own department, project and deadline, on the university's job portal (employment.ku.dk lists pages of current 'PhD position/fellowship/scholarship' vacancies with individual closing dates). The stored URL (a people/PhD search page) no longer resolves (HTTP 500 on fetch, 2026-10-28), and neither it nor the job portal describes a single scholarship with shared terms into which this record could be rewritten. Presenting a rolling vacancy board as one international scholarship would mislead applicants, so the reliability-4 row is retired; candidates should instead watch the UCPH job portal for individual funded PhD vacancies. Kept for audit only; not a discoverable scholarship. university_scholarships row 512 withdrawn; source 2558 (previously the bare ku.dk domain) repointed to the UCPH PhD vacancies board used for this determination.$note$, last_updated_at = now() WHERE id = 377;

-- ═══ 414 RETIRE (employment model) ═══
UPDATE scholarships SET is_active = false, application_status = 'closed', award_amount = NULL, award_currency = NULL, award_period = NULL, award_basis = NULL, living_allowance = NULL, funding_type = NULL, tuition_coverage = NULL, english_requirements = NULL, required_documents = NULL, expected_opening_period = NULL, expected_deadline_period = NULL, rounds = NULL, notes = $note$Retired on 2026-10-28 (cleanup pass 113) as a generic employment-model row, not a scholarship scheme. 'DTU PhD Salaried Fellowships' names no award programme: DTU's official pages (fetched 2026-10-28; the URL stored on this row returns 404) state the Danish model verbatim - 'PhD students in Denmark are considered employees and earn salary, pension, holidays and other benefits'; at DTU the PhD student 'is normally offered a fixed-term employment contract for the full 3-year duration' under the collective agreement (DTU's own salary page puts the monthly salary incl. pension at approx. DKK 34,000 for a student without seniority, April 2024 level). Entry is by hired position, not by a fellowship call: DTU's 'How to apply for a PhD' page directs candidates to pre-defined projects 'advertised under Vacancies at DTU' (or to designing a project with a supervisor), each vacancy with its own funding source (DTU, external grants, industry, MSCA...), requirements and deadline. There is no common award with shared amounts, deadlines or eligibility that this record could be rewritten into, and cataloguing an employment system as a scholarship would mislead applicants, so the reliability-4 row is retired; candidates should instead watch DTU's vacancy list for individual PhD positions. Kept for audit only; not a discoverable scholarship. university_scholarships row 447 withdrawn; source 2511 (previously the bare dtu.dk domain) repointed to DTU's official PhD employment-benefits page used for this determination.$note$, last_updated_at = now() WHERE id = 414;

